+- **Four rules, one per module.**
+  - **Module 1.** Everything is computed at an **identified evaluation** — a
+    `(snapshot, as-of instant)` pair — and over an unchanged snapshot claims
+    may only **degrade**; statuses never improve on their own
+    (RFC2-3/RFC2-4).
+  - **Module 2.** **Inference can only challenge, never confirm.**
+    - Admission checks the *presence* of the floor criteria rather than their
+      merit — which is why a machine-minted challenge additionally needs a
+      recorded human admission act (RFC2-8/RFC2-13).
+  - **Module 3.** **Merging code proves nothing.**
+    - Merged work stays "merged, not yet evaluated" until a reconciliation
+      evaluation checks it against the exact intent revision that warranted
+      it (RFC2-18/RFC2-19).
+  - **Module 4.** Every Unknown carries exactly one of **twelve closed
+    reasons**, and only `gate-backed` Observed evidence can make anything
+    green (RFC2-24/RFC2-25).
+- **Two invariants across the package.**
+  - **Contradiction and gap never merge.** A contradiction goes to the owner
+    and is never resolved by picking a winner, while a gap exits only by
+    evidence or by an expiring, reasoned dismissal — the two are never merged
+    in any surface, count, or UI string (RFC2-15, RFC2-17).
+  - **An uncaptured input is an uninfluential one.** A conclusion whose input
+    is missing from the snapshot renders Unknown rather than quietly
+    proceeding (RFC2-2).
+- **How the modules depend on each other.** Module 1 is presupposed by all
+  others; modules 2–4 are independently readable given it.
